1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:03,423
This is the last lesson of the first masterclass.

2
00:00:03,973 --> 00:00:06,874
So today, you stop watching, and start designing.

3
00:00:07,424 --> 00:00:10,283
CloudCart wants a new feature: save for later.

4
00:00:10,833 --> 00:00:14,523
Your job is to plan how it travels from idea to customers.

5
00:00:15,073 --> 00:00:17,655
You will use everything from lessons one to seven.

6
00:00:19,705 --> 00:00:21,006
Here is the scenario.

7
00:00:21,556 --> 00:00:25,247
Customers want to move an item out of the cart, and save it for later.

8
00:00:25,797 --> 00:00:27,866
The ticket is C A R T, two ten.

9
00:00:28,416 --> 00:00:31,019
You must produce one page, a workflow table.

10
00:00:31,569 --> 00:00:35,388
It shows each stage, who owns it, what it produces, and who receives it.

11
00:00:35,938 --> 00:00:38,775
It also shows the approvals, and the rollback plan.

12
00:00:40,825 --> 00:00:42,425
Start with the table itself.

13
00:00:42,975 --> 00:00:44,618
The first column is the stage.

14
00:00:45,168 --> 00:00:48,624
The second is the owner, one named role, never everyone.

15
00:00:49,174 --> 00:00:52,459
The third is the artifact, the thing that stage produces.

16
00:00:53,009 --> 00:00:56,316
The fourth is the handoff, which says who receives it next.

17
00:00:56,866 --> 00:00:59,511
If any cell is empty, the workflow has a gap.

18
00:01:01,561 --> 00:01:03,460
Now fill it in for save for later.

19
00:01:04,010 --> 00:01:04,808
Requirement.

20
00:01:04,808 --> 00:01:05,873
Product owns it,

21
00:01:05,873 --> 00:01:09,135
and produces the ticket with acceptance criteria.

22
00:01:09,135 --> 00:01:10,666
It goes to a developer.

23
00:01:11,216 --> 00:01:11,533
Code.

24
00:01:11,533 --> 00:01:12,804
A developer owns it,

25
00:01:12,804 --> 00:01:14,202
and produces a branch,

26
00:01:14,202 --> 00:01:14,710
commits,

27
00:01:14,710 --> 00:01:15,663
and unit tests.

28
00:01:15,663 --> 00:01:17,061
It goes to a reviewer.

29
00:01:17,611 --> 00:01:18,054
Review.

30
00:01:18,054 --> 00:01:19,256
A teammate owns it,

31
00:01:19,256 --> 00:01:21,660
and produces an approved pull request.

32
00:01:21,660 --> 00:01:23,179
It goes to the pipeline.

33
00:01:23,729 --> 00:01:24,046
Test.

34
00:01:24,046 --> 00:01:25,820
The pipeline and Q A own it,

35
00:01:25,820 --> 00:01:28,228
and produce a build and a test report.

36
00:01:28,228 --> 00:01:30,065
It goes to the release owner.

37
00:01:30,615 --> 00:01:34,071
Notice that every row ends by naming the next receiver.

38
00:01:36,121 --> 00:01:37,550
Three stages remain.

39
00:01:38,100 --> 00:01:38,650
Release.

40
00:01:38,650 --> 00:01:42,837
The release owner produces version one point five point zero,

41
00:01:42,837 --> 00:01:44,142
with release notes.

42
00:01:44,142 --> 00:01:45,652
It goes to operations.

43
00:01:46,202 --> 00:01:51,237
Deployment. DevOps produces the deployment record, and hands over to monitoring.

44
00:01:51,787 --> 00:01:56,182
Feedback. Product and operations read the metrics, and produce new tickets.

45
00:01:56,732 --> 00:01:59,932
Those tickets go back to Product, and the loop continues.

46
00:02:01,982 --> 00:02:03,966
Handoffs are where work gets lost.

47
00:02:04,516 --> 00:02:06,265
A good handoff has four parts.

48
00:02:06,815 --> 00:02:10,229
First, a clear input: what exactly is being passed on.

49
00:02:10,779 --> 00:02:13,765
Second, a done rule: what must be true before it moves.

50
00:02:14,315 --> 00:02:17,558
Third, evidence, such as a test report, or an approval.

51
00:02:18,108 --> 00:02:21,073
Fourth, a named receiver, who knows it is now theirs.

52
00:02:21,623 --> 00:02:24,183
Miss one, and work waits, or gets repeated.

53
00:02:26,233 --> 00:02:28,324
Now add what you learned in lesson seven.

54
00:02:28,874 --> 00:02:33,930
Which environments does this change pass through? Development, then test, then production.

55
00:02:34,480 --> 00:02:39,066
Which approvals sit in between? Code review, Q A sign-off, and a release approval.

56
00:02:39,616 --> 00:02:40,535
Rate the risk.

57
00:02:40,535 --> 00:02:42,636
Saving an item is low to medium,

58
00:02:42,636 --> 00:02:44,474
because it touches the cart,

59
00:02:44,474 --> 00:02:45,590
but not payments.

60
00:02:46,140 --> 00:02:50,257
Write the rollback plan: the trigger, the previous version, and who decides.

61
00:02:50,807 --> 00:02:53,850
For example: if errors stay high for five minutes,

62
00:02:53,850 --> 00:02:56,588
return to version one point four point three.

63
00:02:58,638 --> 00:03:00,281
A good workflow can be traced.

64
00:03:00,831 --> 00:03:03,433
Start at the ticket: C A R T, two ten.

65
00:03:03,983 --> 00:03:06,309
It links to a pull request, and its review.

66
00:03:06,859 --> 00:03:08,629
Then to the build, and the version.

67
00:03:09,179 --> 00:03:10,950
Then to the deployment record.

68
00:03:11,500 --> 00:03:15,255
If customers report a problem, you can follow this chain backwards.

69
00:03:15,805 --> 00:03:18,365
No chain, no trace, and no quick answers.

70
00:03:20,415 --> 00:03:24,276
How do you know your workflow is good? Use this pass or fail rubric.

71
00:03:24,826 --> 00:03:27,173
One: every stage has one named owner.

72
00:03:27,723 --> 00:03:30,667
Two: every stage produces a named artifact.

73
00:03:31,217 --> 00:03:33,692
Three: every handoff names who receives it.

74
00:03:34,242 --> 00:03:38,359
Four: each move toward production has an approval that matches the risk.

75
00:03:38,909 --> 00:03:42,472
Five: the rollback plan names a trigger, and a previous version.

76
00:03:43,022 --> 00:03:45,411
Six: feedback returns as a new ticket.

77
00:03:45,961 --> 00:03:48,052
You pass only when all six are true.

78
00:03:50,102 --> 00:03:51,958
Watch for four common mistakes.

79
00:03:52,508 --> 00:03:55,239
No owner. If everyone owns it, nobody does.

80
00:03:55,789 --> 00:03:59,671
A vague artifact, like the code, instead of pull request sixty-one.

81
00:04:00,221 --> 00:04:03,613
No rollback plan, so the first failure becomes a crisis.

82
00:04:04,163 --> 00:04:06,681
No feedback loop, so the team never learns.

83
00:04:07,231 --> 00:04:09,044
Check your own table for all four.

84
00:04:11,094 --> 00:04:12,310
Now it is your turn.

85
00:04:12,860 --> 00:04:16,743
The new feature is order tracking: customers see where their parcel is.

86
00:04:17,293 --> 00:04:21,047
Create your own workflow table for it, with all seven stages.

87
00:04:21,597 --> 00:04:25,224
Add the environments, approvals, risk rating, and rollback plan.

88
00:04:25,774 --> 00:04:28,313
Then mark yourself against the six-point rubric.

89
00:04:28,863 --> 00:04:31,721
Pause the video now, and do it before you continue.

90
00:04:48,271 --> 00:04:50,724
Before we close, look at the whole masterclass.

91
00:04:51,274 --> 00:04:53,429
You met requirements, and user stories.

92
00:04:53,979 --> 00:04:58,758
You compared the phases with Waterfall, Agile, Scrum, Kanban, and DevOps.

93
00:04:59,308 --> 00:05:02,273
You learned the parts of an application, and who does what.

94
00:05:02,823 --> 00:05:06,748
You traced a change, and learned environments, approvals, and rollback.

95
00:05:07,298 --> 00:05:09,773
And today, you designed a complete workflow.

96
00:05:10,323 --> 00:05:13,672
Passing the rubric on your own is how you show you have mastered it.

97
00:05:15,722 --> 00:05:16,597
Quick check.

98
00:05:17,147 --> 00:05:19,728
What four columns does a workflow table need?

99
00:05:20,278 --> 00:05:22,219
What are the four parts of a good handoff?

100
00:05:22,769 --> 00:05:24,284
What does a rollback plan name?

101
00:05:24,834 --> 00:05:26,839
Which six things does the rubric check?

102
00:05:27,389 --> 00:05:31,485
Objective covered: MC01-O08.

103
00:05:32,035 --> 00:05:34,894
Pause the video, and check your table against the rubric.

104
00:05:45,444 --> 00:05:48,729
Next masterclass: I T and computer fundamentals.

105
00:05:49,279 --> 00:05:51,157
Subscribe, so you do not miss it.
